---
import Solution from '../../layouts/Solution.astro';
import SolutionExample from '../../components/SolutionExample.astro';
import SolutionFit from '../../components/SolutionFit.astro';
import SolutionFaq from '../../components/SolutionFaq.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../../components/DocsHandoff.astro';
import { solutionAreas } from '../../data/solutions';
import { describeServiceHref } from '../../data/contact';
const area = solutionAreas.find((a) => a.key === 'verifiable-credentials')!;
---
<Solution
  area={area}
  heading="Issue verifiable credentials from your register."
  description="A verifiable credential is a digital statement signed by the institution that issues it. A person holds it in a digital wallet and shows it to a service. People get a credential that states information from your register, for example the recorded status of a professional licence. Its digital signature lets a receiving service check who issued it and whether it was altered. An implementation connects your register, a compatible digital wallet and the service that will accept the credential."
>
  <SolutionExample
    eyebrow="Illustrative example: licensing"
    heading="The credential states licence status without the licence history."
    intro="An employer needs to check a professional’s licence status. The professional council can issue a credential with that answer. The credential does not include the full licence record."
    items={[
      { term: 'The council issues', body: 'Evidence Gateway is the product that answers an agreed question from a register and signs the answer. Here it answers the agreed licence-status question. It then signs a credential that contains that answer. The council controls which service may start issuing the credential. It also controls how the credential offer reaches the intended person.' },
      { term: 'The professional receives', body: 'The professional receives the credential in the compatible wallet selected for the service. They present it when the employer asks.' },
      { term: 'The employer checks', body: 'The employer’s service checks the issuer’s signature and the credential’s validity. Then it applies its own acceptance rules.' },
    ]}
  />
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="credential-value-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What you can issue</p>
        <h2 id="credential-value-title">Choose the information each credential contains.</h2>
        <p>You define the question, the source information and the answer the credential may contain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Professional licence status</h3><p>A credential can state whether your register records a licence as current. It does not include the licence number, the exact expiry date or the history of earlier decisions.</p></article>
        <article><h3>An age requirement</h3><p>A credential can state whether someone meets an age requirement. It does not include their name or date of birth.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Programme enrolment</h3><p>An implementation can configure a credential to state whether someone is enrolled in a programme. It does not include the household’s assessment file.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <SolutionFit
    area={area}
    heading="The implementation connects issuance, the wallet and the receiving service."
    muted
    jobs={{
      'evidence-gateway': 'It checks the agreed question against your data source. Then it signs a credential that contains the permitted answer.',
    }}
    extra={[
      { term: 'Credential delivery', body: 'A separate delivery adapter passes the credential to a compatible wallet. Your existing register can stay the source of information.' },
      { term: 'Wallet and receiving service', body: 'The implementation includes selecting and testing the wallet. It also includes connecting the receiving service. Registry Stack supplies verification tools. The receiving service still has to request and accept the credential, check its use and apply its own decision rules.' },
      { term: 'Issuance and operating arrangements', body: 'Agree how people request credentials and how access is checked. Agree who hosts each part and how credentials are reissued when needed. These arrangements are part of the implementation scope.' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>Start with one credential and one receiving service. Test the complete journey, from requesting the credential to using it.</p>
  </SolutionFit>
  <SolutionFaq
    items={[
      {
        q: 'Does Registry Stack include a digital wallet?',
        a: 'No. It supplies credential issuance, a delivery adapter and verification tools. We can help your team evaluate a wallet and a receiving service for the implementation. The implementation must test that the wallet can receive, store and present the credential.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What happens when the register changes?',
        a: 'An issued credential keeps the result of its check until it expires. It does not update when the register changes. The current software cannot revoke a credential and has no status list. You set the validity period and the reissue process for the service. A service that needs current information can request a fresh check. Issue and expiry dates describe the credential, not the last update of the source record.',
        link: { href: '/solutions/check-a-fact/', label: 'Check information from another register' },
      },
      {
        q: 'What does verification confirm?',
        a: 'Verification checks the issuer’s signature and the credential’s validity. It also checks that the holder controls the wallet key linked to the credential. The receiving service separately confirms who is presenting the credential. It confirms whether the source information meets its requirements and what entitlement follows. It also ties the presentation to the current request and prevents reuse.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can we use our existing register?',
        a: 'Yes. Your technical team can connect Evidence Gateway to your existing register or to a read-only extract. The implementation must account for how recent that information is when the credential is issued.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Where can our technical team check the supported standards?',
        a: 'The documentation describes the supported SD-JWT VC credential format and the OpenID for Verifiable Credential Issuance (OID4VCI) profile. It also explains the delivery adapter and the work needed to integrate a wallet and a verifier.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/configure/evidence-oid4vci/', label: 'Read the credential delivery guide' },
      },
    ]}
  />
  <DocsHandoff
    eyebrow="Work with us"
    heading="Talk to us about a credential you could issue."
    body="Tell us what the credential should state, who will receive it and which service will accept it. We can help define an implementation and evaluate the wallet and verification journey."
    primaryLabel="Discuss your service"
    primaryHref={describeServiceHref(area.name)}
    note="The core software is open source under the Apache-2.0 licence. Your own team or a provider you choose can inspect it, adapt it and run it. Or we can implement, host and run it for you, priced separately in your proposal."
    emphasis="docs"
  />
</Solution>
